From 8ace44a3cd8d093191e13cb273886b196dc3afa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:50:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] rctl: advertise hierarchical jail enforcement as
 kern.features.rctl_jail_hierarchy

Userland putting limits on a parent jail (a container runtime limiting a
Kubernetes pod) must know whether they are enforced on child jails:
without the previous change a parent rule only applied to the parent's own
processes while counting the children's usage, so e.g. memoryuse:sigkill
killed the parent's processes when the children used too much.
---
 sys/kern/kern_rctl.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/kern/kern_rctl.c b/sys/kern/kern_rctl.c
index 1b4bc3a97..4e4c5b007 100644
--- a/sys/kern/kern_rctl.c
+++ b/sys/kern/kern_rctl.c
@@ -63,6 +63,14 @@
 #endif
 
 FEATURE(rctl, "Resource Limits");
+/*
+ * Jail rules are enforced on processes in child jails too.  Lets userland
+ * (e.g. container runtimes putting a pod's limits on a parent jail) tell
+ * this apart from kernels where such a rule only applies to processes in the
+ * jail itself while counting its children's usage: there, a memoryuse:sigkill
+ * rule on the parent would kill the parent jail's own processes.
+ */
+FEATURE(rctl_jail_hierarchy, "Resource limits on a jail apply to its child jails");
 
 #define	HRF_DEFAULT		0
 #define	HRF_DONT_INHERIT	1
